From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:39:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): a ghostty pane honours compatibility.allowOSC52
 (KD-22)

The other half of WT's compatibility switches. `compatibility.allowOSC52`
decides whether a program running in the pane may put text on the clipboard;
ghostty spells the same thing `clipboard-write`, and it was never forwarded.
Both default to allowing it, which is why this stayed invisible: it bites only
the user who deliberately turned OSC 52 off and got a ghostty pane that wrote
the clipboard anyway.

This gates OSC 52 and nothing else, which is the part worth being sure about
rather than reasoning about. Measured through the shipped DLL with
harness/hwnd-host, feeding `OSC 52;c;Y2xpcGJvYXJkIHByb2Jl`:

  --clipboard-write=allow  ->  [clip] mime=text/plain len=15 head=clipboard probe
  --clipboard-write=deny   ->  info(surface): application attempted to write
                               clipboard, but 'clipboard-write' is set to deny

and, in the same denied configuration, a selection plus the same
`copy_to_clipboard` binding action GhosttyControlCore sends still delivers:

  [clip] copy plain
  [clip] mime=text/plain len=13 head=OPY THIS LINE

so refusing a program's OSC 52 does not cost the user their own copy.

`ask` is a third value ghostty accepts and this deliberately never sends: it
asks the embedder to confirm through the `confirm` flag on the write callback,
and GhosttyEngine's callback implements no prompt, so it would read as
"confirm first" and behave as "write silently". A unit test pins that too.

The read half needs nothing. WT parses an OSC 52 query and then ignores it
unconditionally, and GhosttyEngine's read_clipboard_cb already answers
"nothing available", so a program cannot read the clipboard on either engine
whatever this setting says.

unitControl 69 -> 70.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp             | 24 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../GhosttySettingsTests.cpp                  | 25 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
index ce2410edd..cbba18e9f 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
@@ -129,6 +129,30 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // clipboard - measured, because that was the regression to fear.
         set("selection-trim-line", "false");
 
+        // --- Clipboard ----------------------------------------------------
+        // The other half of the compatibility switches: WT's
+        // `compatibility.allowOSC52` decides whether a program running in the
+        // pane may put text on the clipboard, and ghostty spells the same thing
+        // `clipboard-write`. Both default to allowing it, which is why this one
+        // was invisible - it only bites the user who deliberately turned OSC 52
+        // off and got a ghostty pane that wrote the clipboard anyway.
+        //
+        // This gates OSC 52 and nothing else. ghostty's own copy path
+        // (`copy_to_clipboard`, which is what WT's copy binding drives) does
+        // not go through it, so denying this does not cost the user their own
+        // ctrl+c.
+        //
+        // `ask` is a third value ghostty accepts and we never send: it asks the
+        // embedder to confirm through the `confirm` flag on the write callback,
+        // and GhosttyEngine's callback does not implement a prompt. Sending it
+        // would read as "confirm first" and behave as "write silently".
+        //
+        // The read half needs nothing here. WT parses an OSC 52 *query* and
+        // then ignores it unconditionally, and GhosttyEngine's
+        // read_clipboard_cb already answers "nothing available", so a program
+        // cannot read the clipboard on either engine whatever this setting says.
+        set("clipboard-write", settings.AllowVtClipboardWrite() ? "allow" : "deny");
+
         // --- Desktop notifications ----------------------------------------
         // WT's switch is the profile's `compatibility.allowOSC777`, and it is
         // **off** by default: a program gets to raise a toast only if the user
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
index 175b406c9..cf3700f77 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
@@ -43,6 +43,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(TripleClickHighlightsTheWholeLine);
         TEST_METHOD(UrlDetectionFollowsTheProfile);
         TEST_METHOD(DesktopNotificationsFollowTheProfile);
+        TEST_METHOD(ClipboardWriteFollowsTheProfile);
         TEST_METHOD(CursorShapeIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(IntenseTextStyleIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(BackslashesAreDoubledForTheWordCharsFieldOnly);
@@ -236,6 +237,30 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
                        L"a profile that turns URL detection off must turn ghostty's off too");
     }
 
+    // WT's `compatibility.allowOSC52` decides whether a program in the pane may
+    // put text on the clipboard. Both engines default to allowing it, so this
+    // one is only visible in the "off" direction - which is exactly the
+    // direction where getting it wrong matters, because the user turned it off
+    // on purpose.
+    //
+    // It gates OSC 52 alone: ghostty's copy_to_clipboard action, which is what
+    // WT's own copy binding drives, does not consult clipboard-write. Denying
+    // this must not cost the user their ctrl+c.
+    void GhosttySettingsTests::ClipboardWriteFollowsTheProfile()
+    {
+        const auto on = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(*_settings());
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(on, "clipboard-write", "allow"),
+                       L"both sides allow OSC 52 by default");
+
+        auto settings = _settings();
+        settings->AllowVtClipboardWrite(false);
+        const auto off = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(*settings);
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(off, "clipboard-write", "deny"),
+                       L"a profile that refuses OSC 52 must refuse it on a ghostty pane too");
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(_has(off, "clipboard-write", "ask"),
+                        L"'ask' would promise a prompt the embedder does not implement");
+    }
+
     // The two engines default this setting opposite ways round, which is the
     // whole reason it has to be forwarded rather than assumed. WT's
     // `compatibility.allowOSC777` is off unless the user turns it on;
